From: Veron <spikes/stage3/patches>
Subject: [PATCH] riscv64-asm: la must not go through the GOT for a static link

WHAT STOPS WITHOUT THIS. rung 8 of the riscv64 ladder, deterministically, for
five runs: the stage-2 xgcc segfaults on every invocation, including
--version. Stage 1 -- built by tcc -- runs. Stage 2 -- built by stage 1 --
does not.

    --- stage 1 (built by tcc): /work/bld/gcc/xgcc ---
      --version            ok   xgcc (GCC) 4.6.4
    --- stage 2 (built by stage 1): /work/bld2/gcc/xgcc ---
      --version            SEGFAULT (rc=139)

THE COMPILER IS NOT THE PROBLEM. gp IS. crt1's startup sets the global
pointer once:

        .option push
        .option norelax
        la gp, __global_pointer$
        .option pop

and in the crt1.o this chain produces, that carries the wrong relocation:

    R_RISCV_GOT_HI20       __global_pointer$ + 0      <- wrong
    R_RISCV_PCREL_LO12_I   <no name> + 0
    ...
    R_RISCV_PCREL_HI20     _start_c + 0               <- what it should be

R_RISCV_GOT_HI20 NAMES THE ADDRESS OF THE SYMBOL'S GOT SLOT, and is correct
only when the next instruction LOADS from that slot. The next instruction is
addi -- an address computation. So gp receives &GOT[n] rather than the value
of __global_pointer$. Decoded out of the two binaries:

                gp set by _start   __global_pointer$   off by
    stage 1        0x9f3a0             0xa0e08         0x1a68
    stage 2        0x79b30             0x7a420          0x8f0

and in both, gp lands exactly on _GLOBAL_OFFSET_TABLE_ + 0x18 and + 0x20.

WHY ONLY STAGE 2 DIES. Counting gp-relative loads and stores in .text:

    stage 1 (built by tcc)         0 of 92156 instructions
    stage 2 (built by gcc 4.6.4) 660 of 51603 instructions

tcc does not use gp-relative addressing at all, so a wrong gp costs it
nothing. gcc addresses small globals through gp, as the riscv64 ABI intends,
so stage 2 reads from a garbage base on its first global access -- before it
can print its own version. Same broken gp in both binaries; only one of them
ever looks at it.

THE CAUSE IS IN THIS FILE AND ITS OWN COMMENT SAYS SO:

    /* use the medium PIC model: GOT, auipc, lw */
    if (op->e.sym->type.t & VT_STATIC)
        greloca(..., R_RISCV_PCREL_HI20, 0);
    else
        greloca(..., R_RISCV_GOT_HI20, 0);

The choice is made on SYMBOL BINDING and never on output type, so every
non-static symbol takes the GOT path whether or not anything is being built
position-independent. __global_pointer$ is WEAK HIDDEN UND -- not VT_STATIC
-- so it takes the else branch. And the code does not do what the comment
says: it emits addi, not lw, so it does not even implement the model it
names.

REPRODUCED AGAINST GNU as FROM THIS CHAIN, under qemu-riscv64, assembling the
same construct with the binutils 2.30 built at rung 4:

    la  gp, __global_pointer$                 -> R_RISCV_PCREL_HI20
    lla gp, __global_pointer$                 -> R_RISCV_PCREL_HI20
    la  gp, __global_pointer$  under .option pic -> R_RISCV_GOT_HI20

The third is byte-for-byte what tcc produced, same relocation type and same
PCREL_LO12_I pairing. GNU as emits the direct form for BOTH la and lla in its
default nopic mode, which is what a static link needs.

.option norelax IS NOT THE ISSUE and it is worth saying so, because the
sequence exists to be protected by it. That directive suppresses linker
RELAXATION; it says nothing about pic. The instruction that computes gp must
not itself be turned into a gp-relative access, which is what norelax
prevents, and that part works.

WHAT THIS CHANGES. A DLL still gets the GOT, because there preemption is real
and the indirection is the point. An executable or a relocatable object gets
the direct PC-relative form, which is correct -- nothing can preempt a symbol
in a static link -- and is one instruction cheaper. Hidden visibility makes
the direct form correct even in a shared object, which is why GNU as uses it
unconditionally for la, but this keeps the narrower change: only the case
that is provably wrong is altered.

NOT UPSTREAM. This is tcc at pin 5ec0e6f8 plus this project's series. The
same code is what mc-tcc is built from, so this is not a defect of the
reference arm alone -- any ladder standing on a tcc-built crt1 on riscv64 has
a garbage gp and finds out the moment something addresses a global through
it.

WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED. That this is the ONLY thing wrong at rung 8. It
explains the segfault completely and is verified at every link, but preflight
5's other result -- a program with a global crashing at -O0 and passing at
-O2 -- is not yet accounted for. It may fall out of this fix, since both
symptoms are gp-relative addressing over a bad base; if it survives the fix
it is a second question.

WHY THIS IS IN TWO SERIES. tcc-microc carries the same patch as its 0006. That
series patches the tcc that MC-TCC is, built from micro-c in the seed job;
this one patches the reference tcc the arch spikes build with. The riscv64
ladder applies THIS series -- stage4-arch-spike-riscv64.yml line 353 -- and an
earlier attempt put the fix only in tcc-microc, so run 85603600334 rebuilt
everything and rung 8 failed exactly as before, with gp still landing on
_GLOBAL_OFFSET_TABLE_+0x20. The bug is in riscv64-asm.c and reaches every tcc
built from this tree, so the fix belongs wherever that tree is patched.

--- a/riscv64-asm.c
+++ b/riscv64-asm.c
@@ -211,8 +211,19 @@
     } else if (op->e.sym->type.t & (VT_EXTERN | VT_STATIC)) {
         label.type.t = VT_VOID | VT_STATIC;
 
-        /* use the medium PIC model: GOT, auipc, lw */
-        if (op->e.sym->type.t & VT_STATIC)
+        /* THE SECOND INSTRUCTION IS addi, NOT lw, SO A GOT RELOCATION IS
+           WRONG HERE WHATEVER THE MODEL. R_RISCV_GOT_HI20 names the address
+           of the symbol's GOT SLOT, and is only correct when what follows
+           loads from that slot. What follows is R_RISCV_PCREL_LO12_I on an
+           addi -- an address computation -- so the pair yields &GOT[n]
+           rather than the symbol's value.
+
+           A DLL STILL NEEDS THE GOT, so the choice is made on output type
+           rather than removed. For an executable or an object there is no
+           preemption to defend against and the direct form is both correct
+           and cheaper. */
+        if ((op->e.sym->type.t & VT_STATIC)
+            || s1->output_type != TCC_OUTPUT_DLL)
             greloca(cur_text_section, op->e.sym, ind, R_RISCV_PCREL_HI20, 0);
         else
             greloca(cur_text_section, op->e.sym, ind, R_RISCV_GOT_HI20, 0);
